[Thinking]
**Clarifying touch events in Unity**

I need to think through the situation about touch events in Unity. I’ll explore why a GameObject isn't receiving touch or click events. It might be due to the collider being disabled, the layer ignoring the raycast, the UI raycast target being set to false, or issues with the EventSystem interfaces. I'll provide several options for the user to troubleshoot this effectively and figure out what might be going wrong.
[/Thinking]

In Unity, a GameObject receives touch/click events usually because it is hit by a **Raycast** or has UI raycast enabled. How to stop it depends on whether it is a 3D/2D object or UI.

## For 3D/2D GameObjects

### Option 1: Remove or disable the Collider

Touch detection usually requires a `Collider` or `Collider2D`.

```csharp
GetComponent<Collider>().enabled = false;
```

For 2D:

```csharp
GetComponent<Collider2D>().enabled = false;
```

Without a collider, it will not be hit by physics raycasts.

---

### Option 2: Put it on the Ignore Raycast layer

Select the GameObject in the Inspector, then set:

```text
Layer -> Ignore Raycast
```

Or by code:

```csharp
gameObject.layer = LayerMask.NameToLayer("Ignore Raycast");
```

Then normal raycasts will ignore it.

---

### Option 3: Ignore a custom layer in your touch raycast

If you are doing something like this:

```csharp
Ray ray = Camera.main.ScreenPointToRay(Input.GetTouch(0).position);

if (Physics.Raycast(ray, out RaycastHit hit))
{
    // touched object
}
```

Use a layer mask to exclude that object:

```csharp
int layerMask = ~LayerMask.GetMask("Untouchable");

Ray ray = Camera.main.ScreenPointToRay(Input.GetTouch(0).position);

if (Physics.Raycast(ray, out RaycastHit hit, Mathf.Infinity, layerMask))
{
    // touched object
}
```

Then put the GameObject on the `"Untouchable"` layer.

---

## For UI Objects

If it is a UI element like `Image`, `Button`, `Text`, etc.

### Disable Raycast Target

In the Inspector, uncheck:

```text
Raycast Target
```

Or by code:

```csharp
using UnityEngine.UI;

GetComponent<Graphic>().raycastTarget = false;
```

Example:

```csharp
Image img = GetComponent<Image>();
img.raycastTarget = false;
```

---

## If it is a Button

Disable interaction:

```csharp
GetComponent<Button>().interactable = false;
```

But note: this still may block touches behind it unless `Raycast Target` is also disabled.

---

## Common solution

For a normal 3D GameObject:

```csharp
Collider col = GetComponent<Collider>();

if (col != null)
{
    col.enabled = false;
}
```

For UI:

```csharp
using UnityEngine.UI;

Graphic graphic = GetComponent<Graphic>();

if (graphic != null)
{
    graphic.raycastTarget = false;
}
```

